mango-waf  --  subsystem source material, not vendored as a crate
================================================================================

  Author:     Nguyễn Văn Trọng
  Repository: https://github.com/hoangtuvungcao/mango-waf
  Licence:    MIT  (read from the clone's LICENSE file, which names the copyright
              holder above; the README badge was not relied on)
  Commit:     7f2c30ccd103fe2c36407c12a0fc804d7412b554  (2026-08-10)

  This project is absorbed as source material for four subsystems the fork adds.
  MIT code may be incorporated into an Apache-2.0 work; the MIT copyright notice
  and permission notice are preserved by this entry and by the clone's LICENSE
  file, which is what that licence requires of a redistribution.

  Files ported, with the receiving fork crate named in each case because a port
  rewrites rather than translates:

    intelligence/feeds.go    -> crates/pingap-intel      feed fetch, parse, refresh
    challenge/challenge.go   -> crates/pingap-challenge  token issue and verify
    fingerprint/silent_js.go -> crates/pingap-challenge  the silent JS challenge
    detection/behavior.go    -> crates/pingap-behaviour  per-client signals, scoring
    detection/adaptive.go    -> crates/pingap-adaptive   baselines and calibration
    detection/detection.go   -> crates/pingap-adaptive   the anomaly half only

  Consulted and deliberately not ported: `config/config.go`, whose per-process
  random challenge secret is the behaviour this fork refuses, and
  `intelligence/reputation.go`, which sends client IPs to a third party on every
  request and is out of scope.

  Not carried over from this project either: its rule engine. The fork's own
  engine is measurably stronger — fuzzed, false-positive rate tuned to zero, and
  gated at config load by a pattern-cost check this project does not have.

  ### Attribution header form

  Every file carrying ported logic opens with a module doc comment in this shape.
  It is recorded here once so the five receiving crates stay consistent, and
  `crates/pingap-waf/tests/provenance.rs` fails the build if the donor's name
  appears in a Rust file outside a `//!` line:

    //! <what this module does, in this repository's voice>
    //!
    //! Ported from mango-waf `<path>` at commit 7f2c30c (MIT); see ./NOTICE.
    //! Rewritten for <the local reason it is not a transplant>.

  The second sentence is required, not decorative. A header that only credits the
  source invites a future reader to treat the file as a translation and "fix" it
  back toward the Go; naming what changed and why records that the port was
  deliberate. A file that defines nothing but this fork's own config model carries
  no header — attributing a shared vocabulary would dilute the headers that mean
  something.

